When you move from InMemory to a Database, the biggest change in your config is that you delete the InMemoryUserDetailsManager bean. Spring Security is smart: if it sees a UserDetailsService bean (like your database one), it will automatically use that instead of its defaults.
As a Senior Engineer, I've organized these into the four essential files you need to set up a production-ready Spring Security JWT system with a database.
1. application.properties
This is where you tell Spring Boot to connect to the H2 Database (the in-memory one we discussed).
# Database Connection
spring.datasource.url=jdbc:h2:mem:testdb
spring.datasource.driverClassName=org.h2.Driver
spring.datasource.username=sa
spring.datasource.password=password

# H2 Console (so you can see your tables at localhost:8080/h2-console)
spring.h2.console.enabled=true

# JPA/Hibernate
spring.jpa.hibernate.ddl-auto=update
spring.jpa.show-sql=true

2. UserEntity.java
This is your "Digital Blueprint." It tells the database how to create the users table.
@Entity
@Table(name = "users")
@Data // Using Lombok to save time on getters/setters
public class UserEntity {
    @Id
    @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.IDENTITY)
    private Long id;

    @Column(unique = true)
    private String username;

    private String password;

    private String roles; // Store as "ROLE_USER,ROLE_ADMIN"
}

3. UserDetailsServiceImpl.java
This is the most important change. This replaces the InMemoryUserDetailsManager. It acts as the search engine for your database.
@Service
@RequiredArgsConstructor
public class UserDetailsServiceImpl implements UserDetailsService {

    private final UserRepository userRepository;

    @Override
    public UserDetails loadUserByUsername(String username) throws UsernameNotFoundException {
        // Look in the database instead of a hardcoded list
        UserEntity user = userRepository.findByUsername(username)
                .orElseThrow(() -> new UsernameNotFoundException("User Not Found: " + username));

        // Convert our DB Entity into a Spring Security User object
        return org.springframework.security.core.userdetails.User.builder()
                .username(user.getUsername())
                .password(user.getPassword())
                .authorities(user.getRoles().split(","))
                .build();
    }
}

4. SecurityConfig.java
This is the "Brain" of your security. Notice how we don't have an InMemory bean here anymore. Instead, we register our Custom JWT Filter.
@Configuration
@EnableWebSecurity
@RequiredArgsConstructor
public class SecurityConfig {

    private final JwtAuthenticationFilter jwtAuthFilter;
    private final UserDetailsServiceImpl userDetailsService;

    @Bean
    public SecurityFilterChain securityFilterChain(HttpSecurity http) throws Exception {
        http
            .csrf(csrf -> csrf.disable()) // Stateless APIs don't need CSRF
            .authorizeHttpRequests(auth -> auth
                .requestMatchers("/api/auth/**").permitAll() // Login/Signup are open
                .requestMatchers("/h2-console/**").permitAll() // DB console is open
                .anyRequest().authenticated()
            )
            .sessionManagement(sess -> sess.sessionCreationPolicy(SessionCreationPolicy.STATELESS))
            .authenticationProvider(authenticationProvider())
            // ADDING THE CUSTOM FILTER HERE
            .addFilterBefore(jwtAuthFilter, UsernamePasswordAuthenticationFilter.class)
            .headers(headers -> headers.frameOptions(frame -> frame.sameOrigin())); // For H2 UI

        return http.build();
    }

    @Bean
    public AuthenticationProvider authenticationProvider() {
        DaoAuthenticationProvider authProvider = new DaoAuthenticationProvider();
        authProvider.setUserDetailsService(userDetailsService); // Use our DB service
        authProvider.setPasswordEncoder(passwordEncoder());
        return authProvider;
    }

    @Bean
    public PasswordEncoder passwordEncoder() {
        return new BCryptPasswordEncoder();
    }
}

Summary: What actually changed?
 * Dependency Injection: In SecurityConfig, we injected UserDetailsServiceImpl. This tells Spring to search the database.
 * Persistence: Since we use @Entity, Spring automatically creates the table in H2.
 * Flexibility: Now, when your AuthController calls userRepository.save(), the user is actually stored. When they try to login later, the loadUserByUsername method will find them in the DB.
The "Aha!" Moment
Even though we moved to a database, the JWT Filter logic stayed exactly the same. It still calls loadUserByUsername. It doesn't know (or care) that the data is now coming from H2 instead of an In-Memory map. This is why interfaces are so powerful in Java!
